# dry4lua Tool Spec

Purpose: structural duplication detection for Lua source functions.

Reference implementation: `dry4lua` entry in `tools/tools.lock` (v0.1.1).
Project wrapper: `lua tools/cli.lua dry` (package runner `tools/packages/dry/runner.lua`).

CLI contract:
- `lua tools/cli.lua dry [options] [file-or-directory ...]`
- Supports `--threshold N`, `--min-lines N`, `--min-nodes N`, `--limit N`, `--json`, `--text`, and `--help`.
- Exit codes follow `tools/cli.lua`: 0 ok / 1 business failure / 2 usage error / 3 unavailable.

Native Windows contract (#122, ADR 0066):
- Dry scans on native Windows (PowerShell 7 and cmd.exe); it is no longer in the platform boundary capability table. Upstream `dry4lua.analysis.collect_files(paths)` uses luafilesystem for recursive discovery: sorted `.lua` regular files only, no symlink traversal, no external `find`, shell or `/dev/null`. Discovery failures are explicit errors. Fixes live upstream at `qinyuanj/dry4lua`; this project does not patch or fork the tool.
- The runner reports `[dry] candidate_files=N` on stderr before scanning, preserving upstream text/JSON stdout. An effective result requires N > 0 and a completed scan. Missing paths and targets with zero Lua files exit 1; they never produce a successful no-duplicates report. Duplicate findings remain advisory and exit 0.
- Tool bootstrap network/download failures use `foundation.tool_availability` (#112): exit 3, stderr `status=unavailable tool=dry4lua step=<resolve|fetch|build>`, no scan. Other bootstrap failures exit 1. The platform boundary module and its skip mechanism remain available to other capabilities; dry has no platform skip.
- `tools/tools.lock` follows upstream HEAD. Ensure resolves the GitHub URL and reclones when the installed commit differs; `.toolcache/resolved-tools.tsv` caches HEAD for 24 hours. Invalidate the dry4lua row to force a refresh after an upstream fix. Stale source checkouts pointing at the old Gitea are replaced on reinstall.
- Dry is a soft lane, never part of a hard gate: slim `verify` and `verify --tooling` do not depend on its findings. Tooling tests exercise real scans, including a fixed duplicate pair under `tools/packages/dry/test/fixtures/duplicate_pair` and a non-empty `src/` candidate set.

Algorithm requirements:
- Parse Lua source into an AST.
- Extract function scopes as normalized sub-trees.
- Normalize identifiers and literals while retaining structural tokens within each sub-tree.
- Compute a structural fingerprint for each normalized function sub-tree.
- Compare fingerprints using Jaccard similarity over normalized sub-tree features and report high-similarity function pairs.
- Reports are advisory; refactor only when the duplication represents real shared behavior.

Important: upstream dry4lua v0.1.0 has switched from the previous token sliding-window algorithm to AST-normalized sub-tree fingerprints + Jaccard. Any prior manual review conclusions produced by the old token-based pipeline are no longer valid and must be re-reviewed.

Project workflow:
- Dry is a soft-lane advisory: hits are discussion starting points, never tickets and never gates.
- Run before extracting a function (`lua tools/cli.lua dry <file-or-dir>`); default threshold 0.82. Sample with `--json` before lowering thresholds; below ~0.7 noise drowns real signals.
- Known duplication floor: `host_types` vec3/quat-style data classes are deliberately unmanaged — ignore hits there.
- Structural similarity ≠ semantic duplication.
- Report format: command + target → hit count + threshold → top clusters worth extracting / ignoring → next step.
